10158	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s5","entry":{"id":"review-1084fef656b4513508c98de9bd046dda","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-25T11:29:06.451Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design addresses s5's six acceptance criteria and respects the user's accepted exclusions and compatibility exceptions.","evidence":"DEF §6.5 requires gh coverage, checked results, destructive classification, installation/authentication failures, bounded execution and preservation of existing tools. The design supplies contracts and acceptance mappings for all six. docs/plans/tool-registry.md explicitly records the exclusions, per-job limits, preservation of gh_repo_clone.extraArgs and the two additional exclusions. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md, docs/plans/tool-registry.md]","action":"Carry these recorded decisions into the coverage inventory and performance amendment.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing execution helper and capped-text tools behave as described in the migration baseline.","evidence":"helpers.ts uses 'timeoutMs: opts.timeoutMs ?? 30_000' and a 4 MiB default cap. gh_pr_diff returns '{ number: n, diffBytes: body.length, truncated }'. gh_run_view selects 'logs.code === 0 ? logs.stdout : logs.stderr' and still returns success with '{ runId: id, jobs: payload.jobs ?? [] }'. runShell drops a whole chunk when its cumulative byte count exceeds the cap. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/pr.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/actions.ts, src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Preserve the stated compatibility fixtures and explicitly test the intentional failed-log behavior change.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The typed helper, conversion service and binary execution helper exist with the capabilities this design consumes.","evidence":"callTool accepts 'T extends TypedTool' and derives input/result types from Static of its schemas. convertText accepts a request containing schema and keyValues plus ToolDeps, serializes conversions and permits two attempts on each of two tiers. runShellBytes supports stdin, bounded byte capture, stderrOverflow, aborted and stdinError. The analyzer returned incomplete, largely off-topic evidence; these conclusions come from reading the implementations. [files: src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, src/daemon/tools/convert-text.ts, src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Reuse these implementations without duplicating their queues, retry policy or subprocess handling.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Naming a public-key input 'key' makes the existing approval request show its complete value.","evidence":"Although SECRET_KEY does not match a bare 'key', approval-policy.ts defines 'ARGUMENT_DISPLAY_MAX = 2000' and replaces every longer string with its first 2000 characters followed by a length marker. The design permits unrestricted key strings and repeatedly promises they are 'shown in full'. Long public keys therefore do not satisfy that promise. [files: src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S005/LLD.md]","action":"Describe the existing truncation accurately, or obtain an explicit sc4 change if complete key display is required; adjust the approval test accordingly.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The codespace-create contract prevents every permission prompt while keeping defaultPermissions optional.","evidence":"The installed gh 2.46.0 help states '--default-permissions do not prompt to accept additional permissions requested by the codespace'. GhOpContracts instead offers 'defaultPermissions?' and does not require or unconditionally inject this flag. The contract therefore permits a command path that requests interactive permission acceptance, contradicting its no-prompts invariant and potentially failing unattended creation. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S005/LLD.md]","action":"Specify a deterministic policy for additional permissions. Require or inject --default-permissions where appropriate, or explicitly reject cases needing interactive acceptance, and test that policy.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design preserves the existing clone and release-edit inputs while explicitly changing their approval classification.","evidence":"ghRepoCloneTool declares extraArgs as string[] and forwards it using 'argv.push('--', ...extra)'; its current approval is common. ghReleaseEditTool declares newTag and passes it with '--tag', also under common approval. The proposed schemas retain both inputs and intentionally make those calls destructive. The existing approval snapshot test compares serialized input schemas, so the planned exception mechanism is relevant. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/repo.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/release.ts, src/daemon/tools/__tests__/approval-class-mapping.test.ts]","action":"Keep old-input execution tests separate from schema serialization checks, as proposed.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design consumes shared executor contracts without redesigning their ownership, and prose adequately describes the relevant relationships.","evidence":"The actual executor owns input validation, approval, output validation and subsequent hooks; callTool delegates to it by registered id. The design confines s5 to gh definitions, classification, execution outcomes and conversion calls, leaving shared infrastructure to s1–s3 and caller migration to s7. Its contract table and execution postconditions identify these dependency directions and per-tool ownership. It introduces no persistent domain relationship needing an ERD and supplies no linked visual companion requiring rendering verification. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md]","action":"Keep changes within the gh family and the explicitly named registry compatibility tests.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The shared subprocess result exposes the failure distinctions required by the proposed gh failure mapping.","evidence":"runShellBytes records spawnError, timedOut, aborted, signal, stdinError, stderrOverflow and stdoutTruncated separately. It kills the process group on timeout, abort and stderr overflow, and captures stdin errors through the stream's error event. These fields support the design's explicit precedence and conservative unknown-outcome messages without treating a successful pipe write as proof of remote application. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Implement the mapping with the proposed combined-flag fixtures and retain the distinction between subprocess completion and remote outcome.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every upstream s5 acceptance criterion has a proposed proving test.","evidence":"Comparing DEF §6.5 against the acceptance table shows mappings for all six criteria: coverage/input inventories, output validation and typed calls, approval resolution/exchange, installation/authentication failures, timeout handling, and compatibility. The snapshot and approval-class tests already exist and enforce the constraints the design proposes to extend. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S005/LLD.md, src/daemon/tools/__tests__/approval-class-mapping.test.ts]","action":"Retain all six mappings while correcting the parser-check procedure.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.2","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Keeping only command paths and flag names, then appending --help, safely validates generated argv against the real CLI.","evidence":"I reproduced the prescribed transformation with installed gh 2.46.0. 'gh search repos --limit --help' fails with 'invalid argument \"--help\" for \"-L, --limit\" flag'; 'gh search repos --json --help' fails with 'Unknown JSON field: \"--help\"'. Value-taking flags consume the appended help token. Thus valid generated commands fail the check, and the transformation does not reliably select help-only execution. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S005/LLD.md]","action":"Preserve safe representative values for value-taking flags, including fixed JSON field lists, and ensure --help remains an independently parsed option. Verify the harness cannot execute a command body before applying it to mutating operations.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"}],"counts":{"high":2,"med":1,"low":7,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-25T11:29:06.451Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
